WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.567
This self join uses the admissions table twice so we can compare an earlier admission with a later admission

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:13.027
for the same patient. The patient IDs must match, because we only want admissions for the same person.

00:00:13.027 --> 00:00:19.427
We also create an order between the two rows so that one admission plays the role of earlier encounter and the

00:00:19.427 --> 00:00:22.062
other plays the role of later encounter.

00:00:22.062 --> 00:00:24.687
That prevents reverse duplicates.

00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:31.127
Then we use the dates to keep cases where the later admission occurred within a meaningful time window after

00:00:31.127 --> 00:00:36.057
discharge. This type of logic is common in healthcare analytics when studying readmissions, follow-up visits,

00:00:36.057 --> 00:00:38.957
or repeat procedures.

00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:44.537
The same approach could be used in retail to compare a customer's first purchase with a later purchase,

00:00:44.537 --> 00:00:49.047
or in cybersecurity to compare repeated login events for the same account.

00:00:49.047 --> 00:00:54.022
Aliases are especially important here because both rows come from the same table.

00:00:54.022 --> 00:00:58.492
Without clear role names, the query becomes very hard to read.

00:00:58.492 --> 00:01:03.452
When building a self join, always define the roles in plain language first:

00:01:03.452 --> 00:01:05.987
earlier admission and later admission.

00:01:05.987 --> 00:01:08.248
Then translate those roles into SQL.
